libmpeg2 0.5.1 as mpegv.library builds it
==========================================

This directory is libmpeg2 0.5.1 (GPL-2.0-or-later; COPYING, AUTHORS),
from https://github.com/cisco-open-source/libmpeg2: its C decoder only
(libmpeg2/*.c and their headers; not the MMX, AltiVec, Alpha, VIS or ARM
files, nor libmpeg2convert, libvo or the programs). It was added to
mpegv.library unchanged (git: "third_party/libmpeg2: libmpeg2 0.5.1,
unchanged"); these are mpegv.library's changes since, each marked
"mpegv:" where it is made. They are GPL-3.0-or-later, (c) 2026 Brian
King, as mpegv.library is (its LICENSE).

The build has no configure script: mpegv.library's src/config.h says what
the 68k (and the build machine, for the tests) is.

Changes that alter no picture (14 November 2026)
-------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/alloc.c: its memory from mpegv.library's mpv_malloc and
  mpv_free (exec's AllocVec on the Amiga), not the C library's.

- libmpeg2/decode.c: the chunk buffer 256 KB, not 1194 KB. It holds one
  slice (or header) at a time; a DVD's largest whole picture (MP@ML's
  buffer) is 229 KB. A megabyte less a decoder.

- include/mpeg2.h, libmpeg2/header.c: mpeg2_sequence_t keeps the
  sequence header's aspect ratio and frame rate codes as stored
  (aspect_code, rate_code), for mpegv's MpvGetInfo.

The IDCT made quicker, the same to the bit (14 November 2026)
-------------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/idct.c: mpegv_idct_copy and mpegv_idct_add, chosen by
  mpeg2_idct_init in place of libmpeg2's C (which stays, and is what
  MPEGV_PLAIN builds). The same rows, columns, sums and shifts; only what
  a block needs is done: zero rows passed over, DC-only rows filled, rows
  with only the four lower frequencies (or only MPEG-2's mismatch
  coefficient) with fewer products; the columns from the rows used only
  (row 0 alone, rows 0/1/4/5, those and row 7, or all), clipped straight
  into the picture; a DC-only block as one value; the products MULS.W
  (16 bits by 16) on a 68k, each butterfly as its four products.
  tests/idct_test.c checks it against libmpeg2's C on random blocks of
  every kind (and on a 68020 under vamos), and libmpeg2's IEEE 1180
  accuracy.

Motion compensation four pixels at a time, the same to the bit (14 November 2026)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/motion_comp.c, libmpeg2/mpeg2_internal.h: mpeg2_mc_mpegv, the
  sixteen prediction functions (put and average; whole, half across,
  half down, half both ways; 16 and 8 wide) a longword at a time, chosen
  by mpeg2_mc_init in place of libmpeg2's C (kept for MPEGV_PLAIN). Each
  byte's average as libmpeg2's: (a + b + 1) >> 1 as (a | b) - (((a ^ b) &
  0xfe) >> 1), (a + b + c + d + 2) >> 2 from the top six bits' sum and
  the low two bits' (and 2) apart. A longword is read at any address (a
  68020 or later allows it); on the build machine through memcpy-like
  unaligned access. tests/mc_test.c checks every function against
  libmpeg2's C at every alignment.

The bitstream's 16 bits as one word (14 November 2026)
------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/vlc.h: GETWORD reads the next two bytes as one (big-endian)
  word on a 68020 or later (any address), not as two bytes shifted
  together. The same bits.

The IDCT's columns written by hand for the 68k (15 November 2026)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/idct_68k.s (new), libmpeg2/idct.c: on a 68020 or later the
  columns of mpegv's IDCT (mv_cols: rows 0/1/4/5, those and row 7, or
  all; put or added into the picture) are hand-written 68k: the four even
  results kept in a4-a6 and d2, the odd in data registers, one MULS.W a
  product, no spills; the same sums and shifts as the C (which the build
  machine still uses), so the same pixels. A hand-written row pass was
  tried and gave nothing over gcc's, so the rows stay C.

MPEG-2's mismatch row done once (15 November 2026)
--------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/idct.c, libmpeg2/idct_68k.s: when a block's last row is only
  MPEG-2's mismatch coefficient (+16 or -16 at position 63, as in almost
  every MPEG-2 block), that row's transform and its two products in each
  column come from tables made in mpeg2_idct_init (mv_row_7's results):
  with rows 0, 1, 4, 5 otherwise, the products go into rows 2 and 3 for
  the new MV_LOW7M columns (mv_cols68k_<copy|add>_low7m), else the row
  is copied in whole. The same sums, so the same pixels.
- libmpeg2/slice.c, libmpeg2/idct.c: MPEG-1's blocks go to their own
  IDCT entries (mpegv_idct_copy1, mpegv_idct_add1: decoder->mpeg1 chooses
  at each block), which do not look for the mismatch row.

Half-pel both ways: each row's sums once (15 November 2026)
-----------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/motion_comp.c: MC_put_xy and MC_avg_xy (16 and 8 wide) work
  down a strip four pixels wide, each reference row's horizontal pair
  sums (top six bits, low two bits) made once and used for the output
  rows above and below it: two longword reads per output longword, not
  four. The same bytes (tests/mc_test.c).

Coefficients' dequantising and sign (15 November 2026)
------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/slice.c: each coefficient's (2 * level + 1) or level times its
  quantiser as MULU.W (both 16 bits: the quantiser at most 255 * 112),
  where gcc used MULS.L; its sign by a test and NEG, not the mask
  (SBITS) made twice. The same values; the escape codes' rare paths are
  as they were.

The start-code scan four bytes at a time (15 November 2026)
-----------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/decode.c: copy_chunk and skip_chunk take four bytes at once
  (a longword read, and for copy_chunk written) while they cannot end a
  start code: none of them zero, and the last two bytes before them not
  both zero (nor 00 00 01). Otherwise byte by byte as before. The same
  chunks, the same state.

Positions times strides as 16-bit products (15 November 2026)
-------------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/slice.c: in the 4:2:0 motion macros (frame, field, dual
  prime, zero), a row position times the stride as MULS.W (both 16
  bits), where gcc used MULS.L. The same addresses.

A faster IDCT, within IEEE 1180 (mpegv 2.1, 14 November 2026)
-------------------------------------------------------------

- libmpeg2/idct.c, libmpeg2/idct_aan_68k.s (new): the rows are
  libmpeg2's, each with its constants times the AAN prescale of the
  column frequency it holds (f5 and f6 kept at twice, f7 at four times,
  so their rounding costs no more than the others'); the columns are
  Arai, Agui and Nakajima's transform, five multiplies, in 32 bits (the
  values times 1024, the pixel the high word). Each row's code is
  inlined for it, its constants immediates. No longer libmpeg2's to the
  bit: within IEEE 1180 (tests/idct_test.c: peak 1, mean square 0.015,
  mean 0.0004). MPEGV_EXACT_IDCT builds 2.0's IDCT, libmpeg2's to the
  bit, which make test uses to check everything else against libmpeg2's
  own C.

- libmpeg2/slice.c, libmpeg2/vlc_68k.s (new): a block's coefficients
  (get_non_intra_block, get_intra_block_B14 and _B15, and MPEG-1's two)
  decoded in 68k assembler on a 68020 or later, the bit buffer, its count
  and pointer in registers across the block. A code of 8 bits or fewer is
  one look-up by the next 8 bits in a table of 256 (built at start from
  vlc.h's: run, level or 2 * level + 1, the code's length with its
  sign's), one LSL.L taking the code and its sign together (the sign into
  the carry). Longer codes and escapes are the C's sums. The same
  coefficients, i and bitstream state as the C, corrupt streams included
  (a code past the block's end of 6 to 8 bits taken for an escape, as the
  C does): tests/vlc_test.c holds them to it on random streams.
